ZFS SOAK TEST
=============

This directory contains a standalone, destructive stress test for the OpenBSD
OpenZFS port.  It is intentionally not included in regress/sys/uvm/Makefile:
recursive regression runs must never start an hours-long storage test.

Safety
------

zfs_soak requires the name of an already imported pool.  It does not create,
import, export, destroy, attach, detach, online, or offline pools or devices.
It creates and normally destroys only a uniquely named dataset below:

    POOL/zfs-soak-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS.PID

Use it only with a disposable test pool.  Before starting, use "zpool status"
to confirm that every device in the selected pool is one of the dedicated test
disks.  For the current test host, the only permitted destructive targets are
sd1 and sd2.  Do not pass a pool containing sd0 or any production data.

The test must run as root.  Logs should be placed on the root FFS filesystem,
not in the pool under test.  A failed or interrupted run is deliberately
preserved for diagnosis.  A successful run is destroyed unless -k is used.

Requirements
------------

Install fio from packages and build the adjacent mmap regression:

    pkg_add fio
    make -C /usr/src/regress/sys/uvm/zfs_mmap obj
    make -C /usr/src/regress/sys/uvm/zfs_mmap

The default mmap executable is:

    /usr/obj/regress/sys/uvm/zfs_mmap/zfs_mmap

The selected pool must already be imported, ONLINE, free of known data errors
and non-zero vdev error counters, and not scrubbing or resilvering.  The script
will not import it.  For example, after independently confirming that
obzmirror is the disposable sd1/sd2 pool:

    zpool import obzmirror
    zpool status -p obzmirror

Workload
--------

The script creates separate child datasets for:

* Static ordinary-file, link, FIFO, metadata, hash, and sparse-file canaries.
* Parallel fio random writes with crc32c verification and periodic fsync.
* Repeated pathname, rename, hard-link, symlink, FIFO, and mtree churn.
* Repeated zfs_mmap semantic and race tests.

Workers remain active while recursive snapshots are taken.  At each checkpoint
the static canary snapshot is cloned, its mtree SHA-256 manifest and sampled
4 GiB sparse-file invariants are checked, and the clone and snapshots are
removed.  Pool status, vdev counters, iostat, and dmesg are captured
periodically.  Newly reported panics, VERIFY failures, and ASSERT failures
stop and preserve the run.  WITNESS reports remain in the captures for manual
analysis because OpenZFS object classes can produce false-positive cycles.  At
the end, workers are stopped, the canary is checked once more through a
snapshot clone, and a blocking pool scrub is run by default.

Running
-------

An all-day run using the defaults except for an explicit ten-hour duration is:

    /usr/src/regress/sys/uvm/zfs_soak/zfs_soak \
        -p obzmirror -t 10h -l /var/log/zfs-soak

Defaults are an eight-hour workload, checkpoints every 15 minutes, pool-health
captures every 60 seconds, four 512 MiB fio jobs, two pathname workers, 500
mmap race iterations per pass, and a final scrub.  The scrub can extend the
wall-clock run beyond the requested workload duration.

Useful options:

    -i 5m       change the recursive snapshot/clone checkpoint interval
    -s 30s      change the pool-health capture interval
    -f 1g       change each fio job's file size
    -j 8        change the number of parallel fio jobs
    -w 4        change the number of pathname workers
    -n 1000     change mmap race iterations per pass
    -m path     use a different zfs_mmap executable
    -M path     put the temporary mountpoint below another directory
    -k          retain a successful run dataset
    -S          skip the final scrub

Durations and intervals accept a positive integer with an optional s, m, h, or
d suffix.  Run with no arguments to display the complete usage.

Monitoring
----------

The script prints its run ID, dataset, mountpoint, and log directory at startup.
From another terminal, select the newest run and follow its main log:

    logdir=$(ls -td /var/log/zfs-soak/* | head -1)
    tail -f "$logdir/run.log"

Monitor the pool and kernel independently:

    zpool status -p obzmirror
    zpool iostat -v obzmirror 10
    dmesg

Worker output is in worker-fio.log, worker-mmap.log, and worker-path-N.log.
Periodic observations are below status/, checkpoint details are below
checkpoints/, and final scrub output is in scrub.log.  Files named
*.completed contain the number of fully verified worker cycles.

Stopping And Cleanup
--------------------

Send TERM or INT to the top-level zfs_soak process to stop it:

    kill -TERM PID

The signal handler terminates worker descendants and preserves the entire run
dataset, any active checkpoint snapshot or clone, the mountpoint, and all logs.
Do not use "kill -9" for normal stopping because it bypasses this handling.

After investigation, destroy only the exact dataset printed in run.log:

    zfs destroy -r obzmirror/zfs-soak-RUN-ID
    rmdir /mnt/zfs-soak-RUN-ID

Logs are never automatically removed.  Confirm the exact dataset name with
"zfs list -r obzmirror" before cleanup.  Never destroy the pool as part of
routine soak cleanup.
